-- ai-jobs-uae-workers-fear-job-losses-20261106 (885 words; primary keyword in title, first paragraph, subheading)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ai-jobs-uae-workers-fear-job-losses-20261106', 'global', 'UAE',
'AI Jobs in UAE: 85% of Workers Already Use AI, Yet 67% Fear It Will Replace Their Jobs',
'',
'New research published on 5 November 2026 found 85 per cent of UAE employees already use AI at work, while 67 per cent worry that AI will replace jobs, with frontline staff far less informed than executives about employer plans.',
'',
$$<p>AI jobs in UAE workplaces are now almost universal, yet most workers fear the technology will take their jobs. New research published on 5 November 2026 found 85 per cent of UAE employees already use AI at work, while 67 per cent worry that AI will replace jobs. The gap between daily use and confidence about the future is the widest the researchers found in any market they studied.</p>
<p>The findings come from the Oliver Wyman Forum's 300,000 Voices – UAE Edition report, which draws on 5,285 participants across multiple survey waves, including an additional survey of 503 people in April 2026. Most of the findings highlighted in the report come from surveys conducted in 2025. The UAE research forms part of a five-year international programme involving nearly 300,000 respondents across 20 markets.</p>
<h2>AI jobs in UAE: adoption far ahead of reassurance</h2>
<p>UAE workers lead the world in workplace AI use. The 85 per cent rate sits well above North America at 72 per cent and Europe at 66 per cent, The National reported. Nearly half of UAE employees, 48 per cent, use AI daily, compared with 21 per cent across the international sample, and 63 per cent use it at least three times a week.</p>
<p>More than half of workers say the technology is already helping them: 56 per cent report productivity improvements and 54 per cent say it has improved their work-life balance. Yet 67 per cent remain concerned about AI replacing jobs. Familiarity, the report suggests, has not removed the fear. Strikingly, 42 per cent of UAE employees said they would prefer an AI colleague or manager over a human one.</p>
<p>The communication gap inside companies is part of the problem. Only 6 per cent of frontline employees agree that their organisations have communicated a clear AI vision and kept staff informed about progress, compared with 26 per cent of executives. "Employers should make career progression clearer and explain how AI will affect roles, what skills employees will need, and how they will be supported. High adoption alone does not resolve concerns about job security," Dr Mona Hammami, Partner and Head of the UAE at Oliver Wyman, said.</p>
<h2>Satisfied, but not fulfilled</h2>
<p>The report also found a wide gap between job satisfaction and fulfilment. Three-quarters of UAE employees, or 75 per cent, say they are satisfied with their jobs, compared with 66 per cent globally. However, only 25 per cent of UAE employees say they feel fulfilled at work.</p>
<p>Among Generation Z workers, career ambitions are particularly pronounced. The research found that 60 per cent prioritise career acceleration and financial freedom over work-life balance, while 73 per cent believe in the future of their company. Younger employees also place greater importance on direct access to managers and senior leaders, reflecting a demand for feedback and visible opportunities for career progression.</p>
<p>Beyond the workplace, UAE residents are adopting AI across other areas of daily life. Some 80 per cent of respondents have used AI for healthcare-related purposes, compared with 65 per cent globally. Among lower-income respondents, 64 per cent use AI to guide investment decisions, and stock market participation is relatively widespread, with 48 per cent of lower-income respondents investing in shares, compared with 28 per cent globally.</p>
<h2>What this means for jobseekers heading to the Gulf</h2>
<p>For workers looking at Gulf jobs, the message is that AI skills are now a baseline, not a bonus. Employers in the UAE are hiring for roles where AI is part of daily work, and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/uae-highest-paying-jobs-2027-20261105">highest paying jobs in the UAE for 2027</a> increasingly reward people who can work with AI systems rather than compete against them. Government roles open to expatriates show the same direction, as the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/dubai-government-jobs-expats-45-roles-20261104">Dubai government jobs open to expatriates</a> show, with digital and data skills prominent among them.</p>
<p>At the same time, the fear recorded in the survey is a warning against standing still. Workers who use AI only as a shortcut, without learning how it works, are the most exposed if their employer automates the routine parts of their job. The pattern matches the wider reskilling gap: 92 per cent of HR professionals believe their own staff could be retrained for AI roles, yet most firms still hire from outside, as the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-reskilling-jobs-redeployment-gap-20261105">AI reskilling gap figures</a> show. The workers who learn AI skills now are the ones employers will keep, and the ones they will hire from outside.</p>
<h2>How to apply, and what to do next</h2>
<p>Jobseekers targeting the UAE should build a short, provable record of AI use at work: a task automated, a report built with an AI tool, a process improved and measured. Certificates alone carry less weight than work an employer can see. Apply through the employer's own careers page or a licensed recruitment agency, and check the job against the official UAE portals before travelling.</p>
<p>Nepali workers must apply through legal channels only: a valid work visa arranged by the employer, a contract approved before departure, and no payment to any agent for the job itself. No genuine employer asks a candidate to pay to apply, and any message promising a UAE job in exchange for money should be treated as a scam. The demand is real, and the pay is real, but only the official channel protects the worker who takes the job.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Details</th></tr><tr><td>Report</td><td>Oliver Wyman Forum, 300,000 Voices – UAE Edition, published 5 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Respondents</td><td>5,285 participants across multiple survey waves, plus 503 in April 2026; most findings from 2025 waves</td></tr><tr><td>Use AI at work</td><td>85 per cent, above North America at 72 per cent and Europe at 66 per cent</td></tr><tr><td>Use AI daily</td><td>48 per cent, against 21 per cent internationally</td></tr><tr><td>Fear AI will replace jobs</td><td>67 per cent</td></tr><tr><td>Would prefer an AI colleague or manager</td><td>42 per cent</td></tr><tr><td>Clear AI vision communicated</td><td>6 per cent of frontline staff, against 26 per cent of executives</td></tr><tr><td>Feel fulfilled at work</td><td>25 per cent, although 75 per cent are satisfied with their jobs</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: Arabian Business at https://www.arabianbusiness.com/business/technology/67-of-uae-workers-fear-ai-will-replace-jobs-despite-85-already-using-it-at-work on the 85 per cent use and 67 per cent fear figures; The National at https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/uae/2026/11/05/ai-used-by-85-per-cent-of-uae-workers-but-they-worry-it-will-take-their-jobs/ on the international comparisons and the 42 per cent figure; Khaleej Times at https://www.khaleejtimes.com/business/85-of-employees-use-ai-at-work-but-67-fear-job-losses-new-study-finds on daily use and the survey sample.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.arabianbusiness.com/business/technology/67-of-uae-workers-fear-ai-will-replace-jobs-despite-85-already-using-it-at-work','https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/uae/2026/11/05/ai-used-by-85-per-cent-of-uae-workers-but-they-worry-it-will-take-their-jobs/','https://www.khaleejtimes.com/business/85-of-employees-use-ai-at-work-but-67-fear-job-losses-new-study-finds'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
